so one thing I was wondering about is  what was it like for you guys jumping  into this movie where the lead role had  already been cast did that throw you off  at all or just picturing Brie from day  one make your job even easier than you  would imagine I mean she was absolutely  one of the main draws for us because I  mean we were excited about the idea of  Marvel making the Captain Marvel movie  for sure but it was when brie Larson  that was cast that we could see it and  the humanity that she can bring to it  and just a range of emotion that she can  express and and not wanting to create a  superhero where we're denying that but  where we're really embracing it it was  it was definitely one of the reasons  that we wanted to do the film yeah I was  wondering for you guys what did you find  to be the most surprising part about the  directing process on a Marvel movie well  I think it's for us it's the  collaboration which is not weird at all  because there's two of us and we're  collaborative by nature but it's the  size of the collaboration and on our  other movies we're used to working  really with just the two of us and a  very small group of maybe four or five  other key collaborators on this it's in  the dozens you know if if not the  hundreds because there's just the the  movie is so massive and and at first  that's a challenge but it's also really  a blessing because we're working with  the most talented people in this  business and so they know how to do  things you know better than we could on  our own given all of those new additions  who would you call maybe the unsung hero  of Captain Marvel someone you didn't  even know was gonna be key to you while  making this movie mm-hmm oh my god I  don't think that I could choose just one  person because there are so many I mean  if you when I don't know if you sat  through the whole credits I bet you did  I mean just looking at the number of  people who are involved in the visual  effects for this movie just so many  artists and and so a dozen different  companies so many artists and and not  all of them are even on there I mean  it's just impossible it's  possible to name just one person you  know we worked with editors on our movie  for the first time I've edited all our  other films and just learning what that  collaboration can be like and how much  they can add to it and just having you  know two more brains to work on  something together the our visual  effects supervisor Chris Townsend and  also just such a creative person so  excited to like learn about what we're  thinking and then build off of that and  he's such a wonderful collaborator Ben  Davis who did our cinematography I mean  it's been we're gonna take up all your  time you know but I also like to  celebrate cats so obviously my favorite  parts of the movie but I was curious how  did you come to the decision to name the  cat goose instead of sticking with  Chewie you know it wasn't that wasn't a  big it wasn't a big thing it just felt  like Star Wars was so part of the  culture that it just felt like we wanted  to remove it a little bit you know the  nostalgia for the Star Wars movies which  are there so many of around us you know  when we were when we were kids Star Wars  felt like it was something from the  seventies and eighties and I think that  was the spirit of Chewie from the comics  but we felt like we wanted to sort of  just slide away from that's life yeah  and just like the idea of having a  reference that would have been so  specific to Carol Danvers past as her  past in the movie itself being a pilot  in the 1980s when Top Gun was out and  having a reference that felt a little  bit more specific to her whereas Chewie  when the comics were being written was a  very specific reference in time but now  with like all the movies out it feels  you know more contemporary and not so  specific to her past understandably
 

